<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Eastern Rumelia</h1><p class="st-meta">1878 CE – 1885 CE · capital Plovdiv</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Autonomous Ottoman province south of the Balkan mountains, created at Berlin to divide Bulgaria and absorbed into it by a bloodless coup in 1885.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-stefano">Treaty of San Stefano</a> in 1878 had created a large <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> split it, leaving the southern part as Eastern Rumelia, an autonomous province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Its constitution, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regulamentul-organic">Organic Statute</a>, was drafted by an international commission, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Plovdiv</a> became its capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Christian governor-general appointed by the sultan with the approval of the Great Powers headed the administration, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleko-bogoridi">Aleko Bogoridi</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavril-krastevich">Gavril Krastevich</a>, with an elected assembly. The majority population was Bulgarian, with Turkish, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomaks">Pomak</a> minorities. Bulgarian became the dominant language of government and schools, and the province's militia was largely Bulgarian.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 6 September 1885 Bulgarian activists and militia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Plovdiv</a> deposed the governor and proclaimed union with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bulgaria">Principality of Bulgaria</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> then defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a>, and in 1886 the sultan recognised the Bulgarian prince as governor-general, ending the province in practice.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eastern-rumelia.jpg" alt="Eastern Rumelia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Rössel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Eastern_Rumelia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1878 CE – 1885 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Plovdiv</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aleko Bogoridi, Gavril Krastevich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Bulgarian Orthodox Christianity, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bulgarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Autonomous Province of Eastern Rumelia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1882&amp;focus=eastern-rumelia" aria-label="Eastern Rumelia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5281 678 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eastern Rumelia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5601 934L5564 916M5601 934L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5564 916h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5601 934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5601 934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Plovdiv. <a href="/atlas?year=1882&amp;focus=eastern-rumelia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
